---
import BlogLayoutEn from "../../../layouts/BlogLayoutEn.astro";

const faqSchema = [
  {
    question: "Which PHP version fixes PHP2Shell?",
    answer: "PHP 8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36 and 8.2.35, released on 21 October 2026. Any earlier version is vulnerable. For PHP 7.4, 8.0 and 8.1 the PHP team published source backports, and Linux distributions and LTS vendors received the patches in advance."
  },
  {
    question: "Is my server vulnerable to PHP2Shell?",
    answer: "If it serves PHP over the web and isn't on one of the fixed versions, yes. It affects Apache with mod_php (the most serious case, with exploitation demonstrated before any application code runs), PHP-FPM, php-cgi, FrankenPHP and LiteSpeed."
  },
  {
    question: "What should I do if I can't update today?",
    answer: "Apply the interim mitigations from the official advisory: memory_limit=-1 enforced by the administrator, request size limits, session.upload_progress.enabled=0 and, on PHP 8.5, fatal_error_backtraces=0, restarting every worker. Block multipart/form-data at the web server if the application doesn't use it. None of them replaces updating."
  }
];
---

<BlogLayoutEn
  title="Does Your Website Run PHP? Update Today or Risk an Attack"
  description="PHP2Shell: PHP has released the patch for two critical code execution flaws already being exploited. Which versions to install, who is at risk and what to do today."
  publishDate="2026-10-21"
  modifiedDate="2026-10-21"
  author="AI Security"
  readTime="4 min"
  tags={["PHP", "Vulnerabilities", "CVE", "Alert", "Cybersecurity"]}
  canonical="https://aisecurity.es/en/blog/php2shell-parche-php-hoy-actualizar"
  faqSchema={faqSchema}>

  <div class="my-6 rounded-xl p-5" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,0.12);border:1px solid rgba(239,68,68,0.45);">
    <p style="max-width:none;margin:0;color:#fecaca;">
      <strong style="color:#ffffff;">Critical alert · updated 21 October 2026.</strong> PHP has released the patch: <strong style="color:#ffffff;">8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36 and 8.2.35</strong>. Exploitation is active and, now that the fixed code is public, automated attacks are ramping up. If you run PHP exposed to the internet, <strong style="color:#ffffff;">update today</strong>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    They are called <strong>PHP2Shell</strong>: <strong>CVE-2026-109618</strong> and <strong>CVE-2026-109619</strong>, both rated CVSS v4.0 <strong>9.2 (critical)</strong>. They let an attacker <strong>with no credentials</strong> run code on the server through HTTP requests. Your application doesn't need to have any bug: the problem is in PHP itself, and on Apache with mod_php the attack has been demonstrated <strong>before a single line of your application runs</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this one is serious</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Details leaked before the patch.</strong> PHP had to bring the notice forward to 20 October and the release to the 21st.</li>
    <li><strong>It is already being exploited.</strong> Cloudflare saw the first attacks at 07:43 UTC on 21 October, hours before the patch, and Aikido confirmed real exploitation that same morning.</li>
    <li><strong>It affects almost everything.</strong> The official advisory treats any version before the fixed ones as vulnerable. That includes WordPress, WooCommerce, Magento, Shopware, Laravel, Drupal, PrestaShop and any PHP website or panel.</li>
    <li><strong>The flaw is now public.</strong> With the fixed code published, anyone can see exactly what was changed. Sansec expected mass scanning within minutes of the release.</li>
    <li><strong>Organisations are switching services off.</strong> In the Netherlands, Tweakers, the Tweede Kamer and the NCSC took websites offline as a precaution.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Is your server affected?</h2>

  <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm" style="border-collapse:collapse;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.15);">
          <th style="padding:10px;text-align:left;color:#c4b5fd;">Your PHP version</th>
          <th style="padding:10px;text-align:left;color:#c4b5fd;">What to install</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);">
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);"><td style="padding:10px;font-weight:bold;">8.5</td><td style="padding:10px;"><strong>8.5.12</strong> or later</td></tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);"><td style="padding:10px;font-weight:bold;">8.4</td><td style="padding:10px;"><strong>8.4.27</strong> or later</td></tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);"><td style="padding:10px;font-weight:bold;">8.3</td><td style="padding:10px;"><strong>8.3.36</strong> or later</td></tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);"><td style="padding:10px;font-weight:bold;">8.2</td><td style="padding:10px;"><strong>8.2.35</strong> or later (end of life on 31 Dec 2026)</td></tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);"><td style="padding:10px;font-weight:bold;">8.1 · 8.0 · 7.4</td><td style="padding:10px;">No official release. Your distribution's or LTS vendor's fixed package, or build from the <a href="https://www.php.net/archive/2026.php" rel="noopener" target="_blank">source backports</a> PHP has published. And plan a move to 8.2+.</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:10px;font-weight:bold;">7.3 and older</td><td style="padding:10px;"><strong>No public fix</strong>. Isolate the server and migrate.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Risk is highest on <strong>Apache with mod_php</strong>, where PHP demonstrated full exploitation. PHP-FPM, php-cgi, FrankenPHP and LiteSpeed are affected too.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do today, in this order</h2>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Take inventory now.</strong> List every server, hosting account and container running PHP reachable from the internet, including forgotten admin panels and old websites.</li>
    <li><strong>Update.</strong> Install the fixed release for your branch or your distribution's updated package (they received the patch in advance). If you rely on the Remi repository, PHP warns it won't have updates today: switch to distribution packages or build from source.</li>
    <li><strong>Restart PHP-FPM or Apache.</strong> Without a restart the vulnerable binary keeps running. The same goes for Octane, queue workers and any long-running PHP process.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the version your website actually serves</strong>, not the terminal one: <code>php -v</code> can show a different version from the one Apache or FPM uses.</li>
    <li><strong>If you can't update today</strong>, apply the interim mitigations from the official advisory. We explain them step by step, with copy-paste configuration, in <a href="/blog/php2shell-cve-2026-109618-mitigar-sin-parche" hreflang="es">PHP2Shell: how to mitigate it if you can't update yet</a> (in Spanish; the config blocks work as-is). And switch off anything non-essential.</li>
    <li><strong>Check whether you've already been breached.</strong> Exploitation started before the patch: look for new or modified PHP files, web shells, new admin users and unusual outbound connections.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    On shared hosting? Email your provider today and ask which PHP version serves your site and when they will apply the patch. Don't assume they'll do it on their own.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

  <div class="faq-accordion">
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>
        <span>Which PHP version fixes PHP2Shell?</span>
        <svg class="faq-chevron" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
      </summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>PHP 8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36 and 8.2.35, released on 21 October 2026. Any earlier version is vulnerable. For PHP 7.4, 8.0 and 8.1 the PHP team published source backports, and Linux distributions and LTS vendors received the patches in advance.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>
        <span>Is my server vulnerable to PHP2Shell?</span>
        <svg class="faq-chevron" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
      </summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>If it serves PHP over the web and isn't on one of the fixed versions, yes. It affects Apache with mod_php (the most serious case, with exploitation demonstrated before any application code runs), PHP-FPM, php-cgi, FrankenPHP and LiteSpeed.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>
        <span>What should I do if I can't update today?</span>
        <svg class="faq-chevron" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
      </summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>Apply the interim mitigations from the official advisory: memory_limit=-1 enforced by the administrator, request size limits, session.upload_progress.enabled=0 and, on PHP 8.5, fatal_error_backtraces=0, restarting every worker. Block multipart/form-data at the web server if the application doesn't use it. None of them replaces updating.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>

  <hr />

